WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.068 --> 00:00:08.610
Today most supermarkets have large frozen food sections.

2
00:00:08.611 --> 00:00:15.637
Freezer cases are stocked with a variety of convenient meals that can be cooked in minutes.

3
00:00:15.638 --> 00:00:20.631
But frozen foods weren't always common—or widely eaten.

4
00:00:20.632 --> 00:00:29.323
It took innovations, war, and clever marketing to convince consumers to give frozen foods a try.

5
00:00:29.324 --> 00:00:40.033
From 1912 to 1915, an American named Clarence Birdseye worked in Labrador, a region in northeastern Canada.

6
00:00:40.034 --> 00:00:44.459
People there often froze food to preserve it.

7
00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:49.879
Birdseye noticed how good frozen fish tasted in Labrador.

8
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:55.649
When thawed, it tasted as delicious as freshly caught fish.

9
00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:00.585
But in the United States, frozen food was awful.

10
00:01:00.586 --> 00:01:04.898
After being cooked, it became mushy and bland.

11
00:01:04.899 --> 00:01:10.403
Intrigued by this difference, Birdseye decided to investigate.

12
00:01:10.404 --> 00:01:18.106
Birdseye eventually discovered that melting ice crystals were the reason frozen food became mushy.

13
00:01:18.107 --> 00:01:23.630
In Labrador, temperatures could dip to -30 degrees Celsius.

14
00:01:23.631 --> 00:01:30.666
At that temperature, fish froze as soon as they were pulled from the water and exposed to frigid air.

15
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:38.721
Since the food froze so quickly, only small ice crystals formed, which didn't affect the food much.

16
00:01:38.722 --> 00:01:45.452
In the United States, foods were frozen gradually and at higher temperatures than in Labrador.

17
00:01:45.453 --> 00:01:50.539
This created large ice crystals—and the problems with taste.

18
00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:59.046
To prevent ice crystals from forming, Birdseye developed flash freezing—a way to freeze food quickly in a factory.

19
00:01:59.047 --> 00:02:06.946
This new method, available by the 1920s, recreated the way food was frozen in Labrador.

20
00:02:06.947 --> 00:02:14.612
Flash freezing improved the taste of frozen foods, but it didn't make an immediate difference to consumers.

21
00:02:14.613 --> 00:02:19.133
For one thing, most homes didn't have freezers.

22
00:02:19.134 --> 00:02:26.462
For another, there was already a popular way to preserve food without a freezer: canning.

23
00:02:26.463 --> 00:02:33.789
It wasn't until the 1940s that circumstances created a true market for frozen foods.

24
00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:39.771
During World War II, canned foods were sent to war fronts to feed soldiers.

25
00:02:39.772 --> 00:02:46.022
At home, civilians were encouraged to buy more frozen food.

26
00:02:46.023 --> 00:02:54.202
With men off fighting the war, many women were joining the workforce, filling jobs that men had left behind.

27
00:02:54.203 --> 00:03:03.670
These busy working women, who had to work and prepare meals for their families, discovered they liked the ease of serving frozen foods.

28
00:03:03.671 --> 00:03:10.419
After the war, freezers became more available, and frozen food was here to stay.

29
00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:18.234
Although convenient, frozen foods were still limited to single ingredients like fruits or vegetables.

30
00:03:18.235 --> 00:03:24.343
They were frozen raw, and the consumer had to thaw and then fully cook them.

31
00:03:24.344 --> 00:03:30.668
In 1953 an executive with Swanson Foods had an idea to change that.

32
00:03:30.669 --> 00:03:41.475
After seeing some metal meal trays on an airplane, he wanted to try freezing a full dinner—turkey with gravy, potatoes, and peas.

33
00:03:41.476 --> 00:03:50.148
A company called Maxson Food Systems had already developed frozen meals to be served on transatlantic military flights.

34
00:03:50.149 --> 00:03:57.082
Maxson had also tried to sell these meals to regular consumers, but with little success.

35
00:03:57.083 --> 00:04:01.559
Swanson Foods thought it could succeed where Maxson had failed.

36
00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:10.452
But it wasn't easy to get an entire meal to cook in one tray at the same time and temperature.

37
00:04:10.453 --> 00:04:14.721
Each part of the meal required a different cooking time.

38
00:04:14.722 --> 00:04:20.181
Swanson solved this problem by cooking each part separately.

39
00:04:20.182 --> 00:04:27.283
Then each item was placed into the tray, and the tray was frozen, packaged, and sold.

40
00:04:27.284 --> 00:04:32.783
A consumer could bake the tray in the oven for about 25 minutes.

41
00:04:32.784 --> 00:04:37.656
The meal would defrost, finish cooking, and be ready to eat.

42
00:04:37.657 --> 00:04:42.809
The next challenge was convincing consumers to buy the frozen meals.

43
00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:48.868
Another new technology helped Swanson sell its new product: the TV set.

44
00:04:48.869 --> 00:04:54.275
Just like freezers, TVs were now in most American homes.

45
00:04:54.276 --> 00:05:00.672
So Swanson sold its new product in a colorful box that looked like a television set.

46
00:05:00.673 --> 00:05:04.889
Swanson called these meals TV dinners.

47
00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:12.944
Not surprisingly, advertisements showed families eating these quick-to-prepare meals while they watched TV.

48
00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:20.588
New technologies, changing society, and clever advertising all worked well for Swanson.

49
00:05:20.589 --> 00:05:25.656
Soon TV dinners were a huge hit with American families.

50
00:05:25.657 --> 00:05:35.201
In the 1980s, as microwaves began to appear in more home kitchens, manufacturers of frozen meals had to adapt again.

51
00:05:35.202 --> 00:05:42.189
The trays switched from metal to plastic, and the cooking times were reduced to minutes.

52
00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:48.071
By then, people's lives had changed too, and they were busier than ever.

53
00:05:48.072 --> 00:05:57.641
Instead of sit-down meals, new frozen foods appeared that were meant to be eaten on the go, like pocket sandwiches and burritos.

54
00:05:57.642 --> 00:06:06.989
Companies later introduced healthy options and international dishes to appeal to the changing needs and tastes of consumers.

55
00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:12.880
Frozen meals have changed a lot since Swanson's first TV dinners.

56
00:06:12.881 --> 00:06:17.765
Yet people still appreciate the convenience of frozen foods.

57
00:06:17.766 --> 00:06:21.357
What will the next frozen food innovation be?

58
00:06:21.358 --> 00:06:28.381
And how will technology change our future eating habits?

